## Root cause 1 — getLoopStats() internal sequential fallback
Read services/gateway/src/services/autopilot-loop-store.ts: getLoopStats()
awaits a primary RPC (bounded ~3s by VTID-03954's supabaseRequest timeout),
and on failure/empty-result falls back to a SECOND sequential call,
getLoopState() (also bounded ~3s). VTID-03964 parallelized getLoopStats()
against isAutopilotExecutionArmed() in getEventLoopStatus() via Promise.all,
but never touched this internal two-step fallback, which can still stack to
~6s all by itself when Supabase is slow enough to trip the primary RPC's
timeout (exactly when the fallback is also likely to be slow).

## Root cause 2 — pipeline/health's unguarded post-Promise.all body parsing
Read services/gateway/src/routes/autopilot.ts's /pipeline/health handler:
taskCountsResp.json() / stuckTasksResp.json() / workersResp.json() are
called AFTER Promise.all has already resolved and the AbortController
timeouts have already been cleared in the `finally` block — completely
unguarded by try/catch, and outside the .catch(() => null) that only
protects the fetch() promise itself, not later body reads. A failed/
corrupted body read (from Node's fetch/undici pooling this route's 3 direct
fetches concurrently with getEventLoopStatus()'s own 2 internal fetches —
up to 5 concurrent requests to the same Supabase host) throws straight past
every existing guard into the route's top-level catch, producing a full 500
for what should be one degraded data point.

$ node -e "const {Agent} = require('undici'); console.log(typeof Agent)"
Error: Cannot find module 'undici'
$ node -e "const {Agent} = require('node:undici'); console.log(typeof Agent)"
Error [ERR_UNKNOWN_BUILTIN_MODULE]: No such built-in module: node:undici
(confirms a dispatcher/Agent-based fix is not safely available in this
environment/Node build — ruled out rather than shipped unverified)

## Fix 1 — cap getLoopStats() total wall-clock time
services/gateway/src/services/autopilot-loop-store.ts: getLoopStats() now
races the whole primary+fallback sequence (renamed to
getLoopStatsUnbounded()) against a single 3000ms deadline via Promise.race,
so the function resolves (degrades to null) within ~3s total regardless of
how many sequential internal steps it takes.

## Fix 2 — guard each response body parse independently
services/gateway/src/routes/autopilot.ts's /pipeline/health: each of the
three post-Promise.all .json() parses (task counts, stuck tasks, workers)
is now wrapped in its own try/catch. A parse failure on any one degrades
only that field (logged via console.error) instead of 500ing the whole
route — matching the existing fetch-level .catch(() => null) pattern.

## New/updated tests
$ cd services/gateway && npx jest test/routes/autopilot.test.ts test/services/autopilot-loop-store.test.ts --silent
Test Suites: 2 passed, 2 total
(full output: outputs/jest-new-tests.txt)

New test "caps total time at ~3s even when both the primary RPC and its
fallback each hang to their own bound" (autopilot-loop-store.test.ts):
resolves in ~3003ms, not ~6000ms.

New test "degrades one field instead of 500ing the whole route when a
response body fails to parse" (autopilot.test.ts): simulates the exact
observed corruption signature ("Body is unusable: Body has already been
read") on one of the three fetches; asserts 200 + degraded field instead
of 500.

